#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198075 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198433 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5329867-1956-5f36-8614-26d483c36efe" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Surface and a Line Given in Cartesian Form"}] :question/id #uuid "89942534-6b03-52ec-8494-830de5eb8128" :question/math-academy-id "e-12558" :question/problem "Find the points of the intersection of the elliptic cone $x^2 + y^2=z^2$ and the line $x +1=y-1 =\\dfrac{z}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of our line in the parametric form:\n![](images/b0/b0a31426fa8f1d55dcc4a94fb7b6544cdba753d879a4b95c67b66662ce43a26e.svg)\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ (t-1)^{2}+(t+1)^{2} & =(2t)^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2}-2t+1+{t}^{2}+2t+1 & =4{t}^{2} \\\\ 2{t}^{2}+2 & =4{t}^{2} \\\\ 2 & =2{t}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2} & =1 \\\\ t & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-1-1=-2 \\\\ y &=-1+1=0 \\\\ z &=2(-1)=-2,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nso the first point is $\\left(-2,0,-2\\right).$\n\n- For $t=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1-1=0 \\\\ y &=1+1=2 \\\\ z &=2(1)=2,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nso the second point is $\\left(0,2,2\\right).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-2,0,-2)$ and $(0,2,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198228 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198349 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a426dc8c-2be0-5e83-93b6-54a634561aad" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Surface and a Line Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/id #uuid "5524aeab-cc64-5da8-bea3-8cd5dd86ebcb" :question/math-academy-id "e-5264" :question/problem "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the ellipsoid $\\dfrac{x^2}{24} + \\dfrac{y^2}{24} +\\dfrac{z^2}{6} = 1$ and the line \n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=4t \\\\ y &=8t, \\\\ z &=2t\\end{aligned}\\right.$ \nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$" :question/worked-solution "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{24}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{24}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2} & =24 \\\\ (4t)^{2}+(8t)^{2}+4(2t)^{2} & =24 \\\\ 16{t}^{2}+64{t}^{2}+16{t}^{2} & =24 \\\\ 96{t}^{2} & =24 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solution $t = \\pm \\dfrac{1}{2}.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-\\dfrac 1 2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=4(-\\frac{1}{2})=-2 \\\\ y &=8(-\\frac{1}{2})=-4 \\\\ z &=2(-\\frac{1}{2})=-1,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nso the first point is $\\left(-2,-4,-1\\right).$\n\n- For $t=\\dfrac 1 2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=4(\\frac{1}{2})=2 \\\\ y &=8(\\frac{1}{2})=4 \\\\ z &=2(\\frac{1}{2})=1,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nso the second point is $\\left(2,4,1\\right).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-2,-4,-1)$ and $(2,4,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198229 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198354 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58427e6a-8e77-5c01-a578-a7dc4b8f8812" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Projection of the Intersection of a Surface and a Tilted Plane"}] :question/id #uuid "b1f59935-cd61-5c59-aa8d-cf7ba7f151bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-5618" :question/problem "Find the projection of the intersection between the ellipsoid $\\dfrac{(x+1)^2}{16}+\\dfrac{y^2}{4}+\\dfrac{z^2}{16}=1$ and the plane $x-z=3$ onto the $yz$-plane." :question/worked-solution "Since we want to find the projection onto the $yz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $x.$ First, we solve for $x$ in the equation of the plane, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-z=3\\,⟹\\,x=z+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $x$ into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{((z+3)+1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(z+4)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{z}^{2}+8z+16}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{2{z}^{2}+8z+16}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}+4z+8}{8} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by completing the square for the variable $z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}+4z+8}{8} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{({z}^{2}+4z+4)+4}{8} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(z+2)^{2}+4}{8} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(z+2)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{1}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(z+2)^{2}}{8} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(z+2)^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $\\dfrac{y^2}{2}+\\dfrac{(z+2)^2}{4} = 1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375281 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198354 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58427e6a-8e77-5c01-a578-a7dc4b8f8812" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Projection of the Intersection of a Surface and a Tilted Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bedafbbd-e54a-52ad-938b-e4181516a007" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375283} {:answer/id #uuid "e3df9bc1-8852-5c21-93cf-182b6a3f0423" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375284} {:answer/id #uuid "37fd0c8d-00fd-5b79-bc23-65236e67ea11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375285} {:answer/id #uuid "84f9bfb8-6f99-568c-bf7f-22853eb560b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375286} {:answer/id #uuid "f98b7114-a923-58e4-8d4e-96f06d8b9f13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375287}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37fd0c8d-00fd-5b79-bc23-65236e67ea11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375285} :answer-field/id #uuid "9359c400-7f2b-5840-be83-0794f0c7588b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375282}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c752f742-ec7e-5448-8e82-63d4405411e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-70553" :question/problem "Find the projection of the intersection between the sphere ${x}^{2}+(y+1)^{2}+{z}^{2}=4$ and the plane $x+z=2$ onto the $xy$-plane." :question/worked-solution "Since we want to find the projection onto the $xy$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $z.$ First, we solve for $z$ in the equation of the plane, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+z & =2\\,⟹\\,z=2-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $z$ into the equation of the sphere, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+1)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(y+1)^{2}+(2-x)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(y+1)^{2}+(4-4x+{x}^{2}) & =4 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-4x+4+(y+1)^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by completing the square for the variable $x$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-4x+4+(y+1)^{2} & =4 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-4x+2+(y+1)^{2} & =2 \\\\ 2({x}^{2}-2x+1)+(y+1)^{2} & =2 \\\\ 2(x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =2 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2}=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198995 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198354 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58427e6a-8e77-5c01-a578-a7dc4b8f8812" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Projection of the Intersection of a Surface and a Tilted Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7fdc9f64-8f26-541f-98f1-b03d749c4ec9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{3}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527555} {:answer/id #uuid "8613b621-16ea-546a-8d02-0eb8e60df3a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527567} {:answer/id #uuid "998c9c3b-094c-5c4d-aa62-4a00049eb5f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1/5}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{15}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527622} {:answer/id #uuid "ad56555e-8cec-57af-bfd0-f0f323686dbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1/5}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{3}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527662} {:answer/id #uuid "b659028c-5f0c-53e3-8081-3554c6331b0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{3}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527680}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad56555e-8cec-57af-bfd0-f0f323686dbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1/5}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{3}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527662} :answer-field/id #uuid "694d6ae8-38c9-5612-81af-0c9ab86e5ac7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197840}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0f6c4178-bc50-541f-bab7-025040917393" :question/math-academy-id "q-70582" :question/problem "Find the projection of the intersection between the ellipsoid ${x}^{2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{3}+(z-1)^{2}=1$ and the plane $2x+z=1$ onto the $xy$-plane." :question/worked-solution "Since we want to find the projection onto the $xy$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $z.$ First, we solve the equation of the plane for $z$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+z & =1\\,⟹\\,z=1-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $z$ into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{3}+((1-2x)-1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{3}+(-2x)^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{3}+4{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{3} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{1/5}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{3} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1/5}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{3}=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338854 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198349 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a426dc8c-2be0-5e83-93b6-54a634561aad" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Surface and a Line Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "091a7477-c537-51c3-beb6-b6c0087afd60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,-4,0)$ and $(4,-2,-2\\sqrt{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336108} {:answer/id #uuid "2d35adef-b006-5c8a-9b76-c78c3812e8f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,-4,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336462} {:answer/id #uuid "9d0116ab-ab93-52fa-89e1-3794249ae469" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(6,-4,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337453} {:answer/id #uuid "cc6a26af-1c45-5c1d-96bb-8937495f64bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,0,-\\sqrt{2})$ and $(6,0,-4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337825} {:answer/id #uuid "e05efb0c-ff11-55c0-a0ca-cc9b06fae2a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,0,-4)$ and $(0,6,-4\\sqrt{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337992}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "091a7477-c537-51c3-beb6-b6c0087afd60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,-4,0)$ and $(4,-2,-2\\sqrt{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336108} :answer-field/id #uuid "79c37ea7-607e-529e-9b16-d72f74ab0c74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338489}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bbbe94e8-02d7-5a8e-87ee-2187671f30cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-70955" :question/problem "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the sphere $(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=16$ and the line \n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2t+2 \\\\ y &=2t-4\\,, \\\\ z &=-2\\sqrt{2}t\\end{aligned}\\right.$ where $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" :question/worked-solution "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the sphere, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {((2t+2)-2)}^{2}+{(2t-4)}^{2}+{(-2\\sqrt{2}t)}^{2} & =16 \\\\ 4{t}^{2}+(4{t}^{2}-16t+16)+8{t}^{2} & =16 \\\\ 16{t}^{2}-16t+16 & =16 \\\\ 16{t}^{2}-16t & =0 \\\\ t(t-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $t=0$ and $t=1.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2(0)+2=2 \\\\ y &=2(0)-4=-4 \\\\ z &=-2\\sqrt{2}(0)=0,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(2,-4,0).$\n\n- For $t=1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2(1)+2=4 \\\\ y &=2(1)-4=-2 \\\\ z &=-2\\sqrt{2}(1)=-2\\sqrt{2},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(4,-2,-2\\sqrt{2}).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(2,-4,0)$ and $(4,-2,-2\\sqrt{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484236 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198349 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a426dc8c-2be0-5e83-93b6-54a634561aad" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Surface and a Line Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20daa437-703f-5c1f-bfef-06c613eae5d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,6,-4)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483620} {:answer/id #uuid "68903205-36b5-5e27-95f6-4876748a4c2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,-2,-4)$ and $(1,2,-6)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483771} {:answer/id #uuid "822195d9-b4ca-5da4-a47f-e77424f2c224" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,2,2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483831} {:answer/id #uuid "9738f5d5-30f2-54f5-b519-04f2dece2bdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,2,6)$ and $(-1,-2,4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483875} {:answer/id #uuid "d1da9504-c5b6-53eb-a566-9e371e654451" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,-2,-4)$ and $(1,0,4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483987}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9738f5d5-30f2-54f5-b519-04f2dece2bdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,2,6)$ and $(-1,-2,4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483875} :answer-field/id #uuid "88c428fa-eb9a-5abc-94ad-cb031dd35e32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484149}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7819f027-d28d-5660-b11c-cd0f95f720fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-70957" :question/problem "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the sphere $(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}+{z}^{2}=36$ and the line \n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=t+1 \\\\ y &=2t+2, \\\\ z &=t+6\\end{aligned}\\right.$ where $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" :question/worked-solution "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the sphere, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =36 \\\\ {((t+1)-1)}^{2}+{((2t+2)-2)}^{2}+{(t+6)}^{2} & =36 \\\\ {t}^{2}+4{t}^{2}+({t}^{2}+12t+36) & =36 \\\\ 6{t}^{2}+12t+36 & =36 \\\\ 6{t}^{2}+12t & =0 \\\\ t(t+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $t=0$ and $t=-2.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=(0)+1=1 \\\\ y &=2(0)+2=2 \\\\ z &=(0)+6=6,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(1,2,6).$\n\n- For $t=-2$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=(-2)+1=-1 \\\\ y &=2(-2)+2=-2 \\\\ z &=(-2)+6=4,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(-1,-2,4).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(1,2,6)$ and $(-1,-2,4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484270 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198349 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a426dc8c-2be0-5e83-93b6-54a634561aad" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Surface and a Line Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19504466-0a10-574f-bb8c-3c8ded8f176c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,1,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483609} {:answer/id #uuid "3402b0ae-2770-548b-bd7e-08caf8efcf00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,1,0)$ and $(-2,3,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483661} {:answer/id #uuid "476cbccb-7f02-581b-b415-12ad49fd0514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,2,-1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483697} {:answer/id #uuid "85f544ac-b2af-53d2-9846-ab93a0ca63da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,-1,1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483839} {:answer/id #uuid "85fe0a6f-92b1-5ed1-85c4-d3ebde706c06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,3,-2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19504466-0a10-574f-bb8c-3c8ded8f176c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,1,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483609} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f0fad20-662a-5ac9-aa37-fdaf4bd1c44b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484104}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bf7120f6-4fae-5d19-b842-ba88886669a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-71129" :question/problem "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the ellipsoid $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=1$ and the line \n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2t \\\\ y &=2-t, \\\\ z &=t-1\\end{aligned}\\right.$ where $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" :question/worked-solution "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2} & =8 \\\\ (2t)^{2}+4(2-t)^{2}+2(t-1)^{2} & =8 \\\\ 4{t}^{2}+4(4-4t+{t}^{2})+2({t}^{2}-2t+1) & =8 \\\\ 4{t}^{2}+16-16t+4{t}^{2}+2{t}^{2}-4t+2 & =8 \\\\ 10{t}^{2}-20t+18 & =8 \\\\ 10{t}^{2}-20t+10 & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}-2t+1 & =0 \\\\ (t-1)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solution $t=1.$\n\nNow, we substitute our solution back into the equations of the line.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2(1)=2 \\\\ y &=2-1=1 \\\\ z &=1-1=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(2,1,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403440 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198349 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a426dc8c-2be0-5e83-93b6-54a634561aad" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Surface and a Line Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bfa91e7-ae5d-50b6-a073-b1f80896d1fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(\\frac{5}{9},-\\frac{4}{9},\\frac{4}{9})$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403442} {:answer/id #uuid "1e88bd92-da6c-54d3-aa02-4b44560aa8ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(\\frac{5}{9},-\\frac{4}{9},\\frac{4}{9})$ and $(2,1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403443} {:answer/id #uuid "44d63414-8671-5d77-9ee7-bc14c9183ad7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(\\frac{5}{9},-\\frac{4}{9},\\frac{4}{9})$ and $(1,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403444} {:answer/id #uuid "1cc17341-08ec-5304-938b-b53f6c9990cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,1,-1)$ and $(1,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403445} {:answer/id #uuid "32211501-cf71-57e9-a95f-ae04c3650a0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,0,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403446}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44d63414-8671-5d77-9ee7-bc14c9183ad7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(\\frac{5}{9},-\\frac{4}{9},\\frac{4}{9})$ and $(1,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403444} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a93a4ae-2761-5844-9076-70de06e6a2f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403441}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b828b568-be9f-5fe6-8a19-4f466a780e15" :question/math-academy-id "q-71131" :question/problem "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the ellipsoid ${x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}=1$ and the line \n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1+t \\\\ y &=t, \\\\ z &=-t\\end{aligned}\\right.$ where $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" :question/worked-solution "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =2 \\\\ 2(1+t)^{2}+6(t)^{2}+(-t)^{2} & =2 \\\\ 2(1+2t+{t}^{2})+6{t}^{2}+{t}^{2} & =2 \\\\ 2+4t+2{t}^{2}+6{t}^{2}+{t}^{2} & =2 \\\\ 9{t}^{2}+4t & =0 \\\\ t(9t+4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $t=-\\frac{4}{9}$ and $t=0.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-\\frac{4}{9}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1+(-\\frac{4}{9})=\\frac{5}{9} \\\\ y &=-\\frac{4}{9} \\\\ z &=-(-\\frac{4}{9})=\\frac{4}{9},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(\\frac{5}{9},-\\frac{4}{9},\\frac{4}{9}).$\n\n- For $t=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1+0=1 \\\\ y &=0 \\\\ z &=0,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(1,0,0).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(\\frac{5}{9},-\\frac{4}{9},\\frac{4}{9})$ and $(1,0,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339022 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198433 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5329867-1956-5f36-8614-26d483c36efe" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Surface and a Line Given in Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e23b552-2a08-59ef-9fb9-7606d9b76605" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,-7,0)$ and $(-1,-9,-2\\sqrt{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336885} {:answer/id #uuid "72b0c1ad-b7a6-56bb-a8b9-f25d72c12366" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,-7,0)$ and $(-1,9,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337060} {:answer/id #uuid "7e2633f5-785e-5512-8199-782356a168cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,-7,0)$ and $(7,5,2\\sqrt{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337173} {:answer/id #uuid "9aa18831-c4dd-54c1-903b-54e761b15816" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,-7,0)$ and $(7,-5,\\sqrt{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337435} {:answer/id #uuid "a3011720-131f-50ab-99fd-6631595209dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,-7,0)$ and $(7,-5,2\\sqrt{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337512}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3011720-131f-50ab-99fd-6631595209dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,-7,0)$ and $(7,-5,2\\sqrt{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337512} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6acb9a6-0188-577a-b64a-3c50dac8a656" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338611}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f3855d60-25f1-5479-91b6-bf5953492318" :question/math-academy-id "q-71955" :question/problem "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the sphere $(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=49$ and the line $\\frac{x-3}{2}=y+7=\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of our line in parametric form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{2}=\\frac{y+7}{1}=\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{2}}=t\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2t+3 \\\\ y &=t-7 \\\\ z &=\\sqrt{2}t,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ into the equation of the sphere, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =49 \\\\ ((2t+3)-3)^{2}+(t-7)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2}t)^{2} & =49 \\\\ (2t)^{2}+(t-7)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2}t)^{2} & =49 \\\\ 4{t}^{2}+({t}^{2}-14t+49)+2{t}^{2} & =49 \\\\ 7{t}^{2}-14t+49 & =49 \\\\ 7{t}^{2}-14t & =0 \\\\ 7t(t-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $t=0$ and $t=2.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2(0)+3=3 \\\\ y &=(0)-7=-7 \\\\ z &=\\sqrt{2}(0)=0,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(3,-7,0).$\n\n- For $t=2$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2(2)+3=7 \\\\ y &=(2)-7=-5 \\\\ z &=\\sqrt{2}(2)=2\\sqrt{2},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(7,-5,2\\sqrt{2}).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(3,-7,0)$ and $(7,-5,2\\sqrt{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484244 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198349 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a426dc8c-2be0-5e83-93b6-54a634561aad" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Surface and a Line Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8caf6956-74b3-5656-bd1b-037cc3f8bd27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(\\frac{3}{2},1,1)$ and $(-\\frac{3}{2},-1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483858} {:answer/id #uuid "912fa47f-946c-5b4e-a27c-271bcf02a4d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(\\frac{3}{2},-1,1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483867} {:answer/id #uuid "bd25a088-02c2-5d4e-87e7-de9467e51d93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(\\frac{3}{2},-1,1)$ and $(-\\frac{3}{2},1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483942} {:answer/id #uuid "e75b5a59-d2cd-5446-ad00-5a6bf90899b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,-2,2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484025} {:answer/id #uuid "fc4a0b05-a807-5c98-96b4-150269a1c9bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,-2,2)$ and $(-3,2,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484077}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd25a088-02c2-5d4e-87e7-de9467e51d93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(\\frac{3}{2},-1,1)$ and $(-\\frac{3}{2},1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483942} :answer-field/id #uuid "35c2262e-fd4e-5f1d-8d6d-8e23cdf0a554" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484106}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "83fca566-7995-55f0-a5c4-c64563aea218" :question/math-academy-id "q-73219" :question/problem "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the ellipsoid $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{12}=1$ and the line \n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-3t \\\\ y &=2t, \\\\ z &=-2t\\end{aligned}\\right.$ where $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" :question/worked-solution "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{12} & =1 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =12 \\\\ 4(-3t)^{2}+2(2t)^{2}+(-2t)^{2} & =12 \\\\ 36{t}^{2}+8{t}^{2}+4{t}^{2} & =12 \\\\ 48{t}^{2} & =12 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $t=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $t=\\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-\\frac{1}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-3(-\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y &=2(-\\frac{1}{2})=-1 \\\\ z &=-2(-\\frac{1}{2})=1,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(\\frac{3}{2},-1,1).$\n\n- For $t=\\frac{1}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-3(\\frac{1}{2})=-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y &=2(\\frac{1}{2})=1 \\\\ z &=-2(\\frac{1}{2})=-1,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(-\\frac{3}{2},1,-1).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(\\frac{3}{2},-1,1)$ and $(-\\frac{3}{2},1,-1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527951 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198354 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58427e6a-8e77-5c01-a578-a7dc4b8f8812" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Projection of the Intersection of a Surface and a Tilted Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0903858d-cf16-597b-9ae8-d85d0bc36245" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,-4)$ in the $yz$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527324} {:answer/id #uuid "3902c414-780f-5eef-ba7d-8eda7aba1b47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,-2)$ in the $yz$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527420} {:answer/id #uuid "78287034-4646-5978-adb5-dcdbf0469d6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,2)$ in the $yz$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527531} {:answer/id #uuid "ab75c7e1-d61a-56b5-b8e3-f5fa5dd5aece" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,-4)$ in the $yz$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527660} {:answer/id #uuid "c6455024-0d8f-5fa3-83e1-94779ed93f71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,4)$ in the $yz$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527706}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab75c7e1-d61a-56b5-b8e3-f5fa5dd5aece" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,-4)$ in the $yz$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527660} :answer-field/id #uuid "f913ee15-c6e1-5a0b-bf56-708c893775f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527904}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "65227920-db32-5f4f-9774-747e0bc8ffd1" :question/math-academy-id "q-73867" :question/problem "For the elliptic paraboloid $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{3}=z+4$, the section in the plane $\\sqrt{3}x-2y=4$ is a parabola. \nFind the vertex of the projection of this parabola onto $yz$-plane." :question/worked-solution "Since we want to find the projection onto the $yz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $x.$ First, we solve for $x$ in the equation of the plane, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3}x-2y=4\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{2(y+2)}{\\sqrt{3}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $x$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{(\\frac{2(y+2)}{\\sqrt{3}})}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{3} & =z+4 \\\\ \\frac{4(y+2)^{2}}{3\\cdot 2}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{3} & =z+4 \\\\ \\frac{2(y+2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{3} & =z+4 \\\\ \\frac{3(y+2)^{2}}{3} & =z+4 \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =z+4 \\\\ z & =(y+2)^{2}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the vertex of the projection onto $yz$-plane is $(-2,-4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527945 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198433 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5329867-1956-5f36-8614-26d483c36efe" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Surface and a Line Given in Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a403057-49da-5281-95e9-2b61e1f6c156" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(4,2,2)$ and $(8,4,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527579} {:answer/id #uuid "8cc1d652-8f64-55d9-a0a5-6794fcdf58b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(4,2,2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527586} {:answer/id #uuid "a19965fa-cbd9-5c65-bf6b-ca13371cc307" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-\\frac{4}{3},-\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3})$ and $(4,2,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527640} {:answer/id #uuid "ce32204a-bd90-5a02-95bb-9e251b7948af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(8,4,3)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527726} {:answer/id #uuid "d755c9c6-8859-50b7-b00f-25201a9e2e8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-\\frac{4}{3},-\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3})$ and $(8,4,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527741}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a19965fa-cbd9-5c65-bf6b-ca13371cc307" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-\\frac{4}{3},-\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3})$ and $(4,2,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527640} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b76cfe8-40cb-5f07-be72-0076c0000916" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527874}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5a9b6c0a-2d84-570a-8860-6a6e61ceeed3" :question/math-academy-id "q-94084" :question/problem "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the elliptic cone ${x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=8{z}^{2}$ and the line $\\frac{x}{4}=\\frac{y}{2}=z-1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of our line in parametric form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{4}=\\frac{y}{2}=z-1=t\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=4t \\\\ y=2t \\\\ z=t+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2} & =8{z}^{2} \\\\ (4t)^{2}+4(2t)^{2} & =8(t+1)^{2} \\\\ 16{t}^{2}+16{t}^{2} & =8(t+1)^{2} \\\\ 32{t}^{2} & =8(t+1)^{2} \\\\ 4{t}^{2} & =(t+1)^{2} \\\\ 4{t}^{2} & ={t}^{2}+2t+1 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-2t-1 & =0 \\\\ (3t+1)(t-1) & =0 \\\\ t & =-\\frac{1}{3},1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-\\frac{1}{3},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=4(-\\frac{1}{3})=-\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ y=2(-\\frac{1}{3})=-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ z=-\\frac{1}{3}+1=\\frac{2}{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(-\\frac{4}{3},-\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3}).$\n\n- For $t=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=4(1)=4 \\\\ y=2(1)=2 \\\\ z=1+1=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(4,2,2).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-\\frac{4}{3},-\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3})$ and $(4,2,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199015 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198433 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5329867-1956-5f36-8614-26d483c36efe" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Surface and a Line Given in Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b8e61fe-84f0-5e8c-b8d0-debe0a505a86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,4,-2)$ and $(3,6,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527428} {:answer/id #uuid "596f5dd6-00ad-5cc4-b4f1-c803276b16e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,2,-3)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527479} {:answer/id #uuid "67eb7249-a648-56ce-b5ed-2207421c0d4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,-4,-6)$ and $(1,2,-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527503} {:answer/id #uuid "6867a5b5-5ca3-5049-85eb-b68832f41cd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,-4)$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527507} {:answer/id #uuid "aa24d1c5-c858-5867-b4c5-389cc27692f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,-2,-5)$ and $(1,2,-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527657}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67eb7249-a648-56ce-b5ed-2207421c0d4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,-4,-6)$ and $(1,2,-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527503} :answer-field/id #uuid "f46b58ea-05c7-5cb0-b42c-c7ee3b8ec141" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197960}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e246b31d-b881-54d7-8871-a92812516505" :question/math-academy-id "q-94774" :question/problem "Find the points of the intersection of the elliptic cone ${x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ and the line $x=\\frac{y}{2}=z+4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of our line in the parametric form:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{y}{2}=z+4=t\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t \\\\ y=2t \\\\ z=t-4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2}+2(2t)^{2} & =(t-4)^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2}+8{t}^{2} & ={t}^{2}-8t+16 \\\\ 8{t}^{2}+8t-16 & =0 \\\\ 8({t}^{2}+t-2) & =0 \\\\ 8(t+2)(t-1) & =0 \\\\ t & =-2,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ y=2(-2)=-4 \\\\ z=-2-4=-6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(-2,-4,-6).$\n\n- For $t=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ y=2(1)=2 \\\\ z=1-4=-3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(1,2,-3).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-2,-4,-6)$ and $(1,2,-3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527915 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198433 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5329867-1956-5f36-8614-26d483c36efe" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Surface and a Line Given in Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "483c1d43-041f-5678-ab9f-50bec83d7f8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,2,-4)$ and   $(1,1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527449} {:answer/id #uuid "69a4522f-18fd-5114-a1be-146db5b750da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,3,-3)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527508} {:answer/id #uuid "983f6f82-3266-53f7-b95d-ce0f1f0ffea4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3,-3,-9)$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527616} {:answer/id #uuid "d3f5a05c-1273-59a1-bc11-b4fb2a55699d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,-1,-7)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527735} {:answer/id #uuid "f7bd770f-a8c7-568c-bde9-a4473018a372" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(4,4-2)$  and   $(0,2,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527788}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69a4522f-18fd-5114-a1be-146db5b750da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,3,-3)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527508} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a8d8bc9-45fa-5d95-aee6-2a55394b5faf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527852}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0c6c3541-8949-51d1-86fa-598cbd047050" :question/math-academy-id "q-94780" :question/problem "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the elliptic cone ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2{z}^{2}$ and the line $x=y=z+6.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of our line in parametric form:\n\n$$\nx=y=z+6=t\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t \\\\ y=t \\\\ z=t-6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2{z}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2}+{t}^{2} & =2(t-6)^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2}+{t}^{2} & =2({t}^{2}-12t+36) \\\\ 2{t}^{2} & =2{t}^{2}-24t+72 \\\\ 24t & =72 \\\\ t & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute our solution back into the equations of the line. For $t=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=3 \\\\ y=3 \\\\ z=3-6=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(3,3-3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375570 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198433 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5329867-1956-5f36-8614-26d483c36efe" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Surface and a Line Given in Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c1a8864-0937-5a93-b9ed-6eb831acf7c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(12,1,1)$ and $(4,-1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375572} {:answer/id #uuid "2a8ad80b-85a9-584c-8568-2b5e5a98f98a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-2,-2)$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375573} {:answer/id #uuid "02e1c7c6-dccd-53e2-9aec-f9596ed92975" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4,-3,-3)$   only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375574} {:answer/id #uuid "6bad198b-b214-5b1f-b47a-16e32392d56f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(16,2,2)$  and $(0,-2,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375575} {:answer/id #uuid "0fd33eb2-e44d-593e-9dc2-78bb8af6fff2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(4,-1,-1)$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375576}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a8ad80b-85a9-584c-8568-2b5e5a98f98a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-2,-2)$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375573} :answer-field/id #uuid "5dd145b8-554f-5187-843c-4effb83714b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375571}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9105e6ec-384a-5ac8-9a8d-8fcef2e66571" :question/math-academy-id "q-94786" :question/problem "Find the point of the intersection of the elliptic cone ${x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}=3{z}^{2}$ and the line $\\frac{x-8}{4}=y=z.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of our line in the parametric form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-8}{4}=y=z=t\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=4t+8 \\\\ y &=t \\\\ z &=t,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2} & =3{z}^{2} \\\\ (4t+8)^{2}+3{t}^{2} & =3{t}^{2} \\\\ (4t+8)^{2} & =0 \\\\ 4t+8 & =0 \\\\ 4t & =-8 \\\\ t & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line. For $t=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=4(-2)+8=0 \\\\ y &=-2 \\\\ z &=-2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(0,-2,-2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527981 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198354 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58427e6a-8e77-5c01-a578-a7dc4b8f8812" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Projection of the Intersection of a Surface and a Tilted Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "187a16f1-6273-54af-9121-31525d2a66bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A circle centered at $(1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527357} {:answer/id #uuid "434dbbdd-05f1-5876-8f5b-b5c7c98fe06b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola centered at $(1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527438} {:answer/id #uuid "73e59cc1-b0f5-559e-80d0-82e39e9654fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse centered at $(-1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527526} {:answer/id #uuid "9c7ccd7e-e4bd-582e-8c22-a8d5b080a385" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A circle centered at $(-1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527630} {:answer/id #uuid "f3e0f49b-43ae-57e9-92c4-31cbcde7a022" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola centered at $(-1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527783}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c7ccd7e-e4bd-582e-8c22-a8d5b080a385" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A circle centered at $(-1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527630} :answer-field/id #uuid "65c7a77f-0b8c-5f82-8806-d3cf2f46ae3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527849}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b35b0faf-35a9-5d63-9b1f-dccf9512ee2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-94951" :question/problem "Which of the following best describes the projection of the intersection between the elliptic cone $(x-2)^{2}+2(y+1)^{2}=2{z}^{2}$ and the plane $x+2z-3=0$ onto the $yz$-plane?" :question/worked-solution "Since we want to find the projection onto the $yz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $x.$ First, we solve the equation of the plane for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx+2z-3=0\\,⟹\\,x=3-2z\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $x$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3-2z-2)^{2}+2(y+1)^{2} & =2{z}^{2} \\\\ (1-2z)^{2}+2(y+1)^{2} & =2{z}^{2} \\\\ 1-4z+4{z}^{2}+2(y+1)^{2} & =2{z}^{2} \\\\ 2{z}^{2}-4z+1+2(y+1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2(z-1)^{2}-2+1+2(y+1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2(z-1)^{2}+2(y+1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (y+1)^{2}+(z-1)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the equation of a circle of radius $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ centered at $(-1,1)$ in the $yz$-plane."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527938 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198354 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58427e6a-8e77-5c01-a578-a7dc4b8f8812" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Projection of the Intersection of a Surface and a Tilted Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "047cfa9f-c80b-5d89-9bcb-ef9cfe5402fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola with vertex at $(2,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527318} {:answer/id #uuid "315608c7-0c8f-5248-a154-6a3c8bbab5d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse centered at $(2,-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527411} {:answer/id #uuid "67b5c53b-ff8a-5933-bb54-e20dd43b9c05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola centered at $(2,-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527501} {:answer/id #uuid "7bc3578e-cd41-50cb-9e4f-3bc653da0f6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse centered at $(3,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527541} {:answer/id #uuid "c9b5de5c-64b5-5024-9665-54c86e6326ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola centered at $(3,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527712}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67b5c53b-ff8a-5933-bb54-e20dd43b9c05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola centered at $(2,-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527501} :answer-field/id #uuid "f606a9bd-6226-593a-a606-90fd2f2de1fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527903}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4b5ee114-4f6d-532e-ba84-9309871f16a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-94952" :question/problem "Which of the following best describes the projection of the intersection between the elliptic cone ${x}^{2}+2(y-1)^{2}=6(z+3)^{2}$ and the plane $x+y-4=0$ onto the $yz$-plane?" :question/worked-solution "Since we want to find the projection onto the $yz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $x.$ First, we solve the equation of the plane for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx+y-4=0\\,⟹\\,x=4-y\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $x$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4-y)^{2}+2(y-1)^{2} & =6(z+3)^{2} \\\\ 16-8y+{y}^{2}+2({y}^{2}-2y+1) & =6(z+3)^{2} \\\\ 16-8y+{y}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-4y+2 & =6(z+3)^{2} \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-12y+18 & =6(z+3)^{2} \\\\ 3({y}^{2}-4y+4)-12+18 & =6(z+3)^{2} \\\\ 3(y-2)^{2}+6 & =6(z+3)^{2} \\\\ 6(z+3)^{2}-3(y-2)^{2} & =6 \\\\ (z+3)^{2}-\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the equation of a hyperbola centered at $(2,-3)$ in the $yz$-plane."}]}
